title: Installation

!! Requirements

# ''Node.js'' (v24 or later)
# ''TiddlyWiki'' installed globally: `npm install -g tiddlywiki`
# ''Claude Code'' (CLI, VS Code extension, or desktop app)

!! Plugin Setup

tw-mcp comes as two plugins, `wikilabs/tw-mcp` and `wikilabs/tw-mcp-core`, and neither is part of TiddlyWiki core. You need to download them and tell TiddlyWiki where to find them.

!!! 1. Get the plugins

''Option A — with git:''

```
git clone https://github.com/wikilabs/plugins.git wikilabs-plugins
```

''Option B — without git:''

Download and unzip https://github.com/wikilabs/plugins/archive/refs/heads/master.zip

Rename the extracted folder from `plugins-master` to `wikilabs-plugins` (or any name you like).

!!! 2. Set `TIDDLYWIKI_PLUGIN_PATH`

This environment variable tells TiddlyWiki where to look for third-party plugins. Point it at the cloned directory.

; Linux / macOS
: Add to `~/.bashrc` or `~/.zshrc`:

```
export TIDDLYWIKI_PLUGIN_PATH="/path/to/your/plugins"
```

; Windows
: Set via System Environment Variables or in your terminal profile:

```
set TIDDLYWIKI_PLUGIN_PATH=C:\path\to\your\plugins
```

The path must point to the directory that ''contains'' the `wikilabs/` folder (not to `wikilabs/` itself).

!!! 3. Add the plugins to your wiki

Add both plugins to the `plugins` list in your wiki's `tiddlywiki.info`. `wikilabs/tw-mcp` depends on `wikilabs/tw-mcp-core`, and TiddlyWiki does not load a dependency by itself.

```json
"plugins": [
	"wikilabs/tw-mcp-core",
	"wikilabs/tw-mcp"
]
```

Optional: add `wikilabs/devtools` for browser tooltips and a context menu that trace rendered elements to their source. See [[Source Position Tracking]].

!!! 4. Verify

Run from your wiki directory:

```
cd /your/wiki/dir
tiddlywiki . --mcp
```

If it works, you see a line containing `Server started as PRIMARY`, and the server then waits for an MCP client on stdin. Stop it with Ctrl+C.

If TiddlyWiki cannot find the plugins, you will see:

> ''Warning: Cannot find plugin 'wikilabs/tw-mcp-core' ''
> ''Warning: Cannot find plugin 'wikilabs/tw-mcp' ''
> ''Error: Unknown command: mcp''

Double-check that `TIDDLYWIKI_PLUGIN_PATH` is set correctly.

If `wikilabs/tw-mcp-core` is missing from the `plugins` list, the server stops with ''Error: --mcp needs the wikilabs/tw-mcp-core plugin''.

!! Claude Code Configuration

Add a `.mcp.json` file to your wiki directory:

```json
{
	"mcpServers": {
		"tiddlywiki": {
			"type": "stdio",
			"command": "tiddlywiki",
			"args": [
				".",
				"--mcp",
				"readwrite",
				"label=tw-mcp"
			]
		}
	}
}
```

Then start Claude Code from that directory. The MCP server will be available automatically.

!!! Pre-approve the read tools

Claude Code asks before each tool call. To let it read without asking, add a `.claude/settings.json` to the wiki directory, or `.claude/settings.local.json` to keep the list to yourself:

```json
{
	"permissions": {
		"allow": [
			"mcp__tiddlywiki__get_tiddler",
			"mcp__tiddlywiki__list_tiddlers",
			"mcp__tiddlywiki__run_filter",
			"mcp__tiddlywiki__render_tiddler",
			"mcp__tiddlywiki__get_wiki_info"
		]
	}
}
```

`tiddlywiki` is the server's name in `.mcp.json`. `mcp__tiddlywiki__*` would allow every tool, the write tools included.

!!! Alternative: Set the plugin path in `.mcp.json`

Instead of a global environment variable, you can set `TIDDLYWIKI_PLUGIN_PATH` per-server using the `env` field:

```json
{
	"mcpServers": {
		"tiddlywiki": {
			"type": "stdio",
			"command": "tiddlywiki",
			"args": [
				".",
				"--mcp",
				"readwrite",
				"label=tw-mcp"
			],
			"env": {
				"TIDDLYWIKI_PLUGIN_PATH": "/path/to/your/plugins"
			}
		}
	}
}
```

This sets the variable only for the MCP server process, without requiring it in your shell profile.